\section{Printing}{

The path goes out through \code{\link[=cat]{cat()}}, so it lands on \code{stdout} and knitr renders
it into the document as ordinary chunk output — the point being that a
rendered PDF states on its face which files that render produced.

\code{\link[=message]{message()}} was the alternative and is rejected. Its output does reach a
knitted document by default, but it is fragile in exactly the place this is
meant to be useful: \code{message: false} in a document's YAML is the customary
way to hide \code{library()} startup chatter, and it would silence this record
along with it. Nothing is conventionally set that would suppress \code{stdout}.
\code{\link[=print]{print()}} is rejected too, on cosmetics — it would put \verb{[1]} and quotes
around the path in the rendered output.

The cost of \code{stdout} is that the announcement is part of the value stream:
\code{\link[=capture.output]{capture.output()}} will pick it up, as will a shell redirect of an \code{Rscript}
run. Turn it off for a call with \code{print = FALSE}, or use the quiet
shorthands below.

The path is reported exactly once: through \code{stdout} when \code{print = TRUE}, and
otherwise by the ordinary visible return value. That is why \code{print = TRUE}
returns \strong{invisibly} — a visible return would print the path a second time
anywhere that auto-prints, so a bare \code{dpp('fig.png')} in a chunk would
render two identical lines. Invisibility affects auto-printing only:
\code{p <- dpp('fig.png')} and \code{ggsave(plot, dpp('fig.png'))} behave the same
either way.

The announcement reports the path that was \strong{built}, not one that was
written. Nothing here touches the disk, so if the surrounding
\code{\link[ggplot2:ggsave]{ggplot2::ggsave()}} or render call then fails, the path was still announced.
Read it as "this is the name the run intended", which is what makes it
useful in a log.
}

